 <h3 id="does-duke-energy-pay-for-the-charger">Does Duke Energy pay for the charger?</h3>
 <p>No. Duke's Charger Prep Credit covers the prep work: wiring, conduit, the outlet, and panel or breaker work. Duke's own page says the charger hardware, mounting and permit fees don't count.</p>
 <h3 id="are-these-installers-licensed">Are these installers licensed?</h3>
-<p>We show a license number only when the business publishes one on its own site, and we're checking each against the South Carolina Department of Labor, Licensing and Regulation (LLR) lookup. Before you hire anyone, ask for their license number and proof of insurance.</p>
+<p>We show a license number when the business publishes one on its own site or when we found an active license on the South Carolina Department of Labor, Licensing and Regulation (LLR) lookup, checked Oct 26, 2026. Before you hire anyone, ask for their license number, look it up on the LLR website, and ask for proof of insurance.</p>
 <h3 id="can-i-charge-from-a-regular-outlet-instead">Can I charge from a regular outlet instead?</h3>
 <p>Many EVs come with a cord that plugs into a standard 120-volt outlet. That's Level 1 charging. It's slow, but it can be enough if you drive short distances and park overnight. Most people who install a charger want Level 2, which needs a 240-volt circuit.</p>
