 <h2 id="what-about-tesla-certified-installers">What about "Tesla Certified" installers?</h2>
-<p>Tesla keeps its own list of certified installers on its website. Jaymarc Electric says on its site that it is a certified Tesla Wall Connector installer. We haven't confirmed that against Tesla's list yet, so treat it as the installer's own claim. Any licensed electrician can install a Wall Connector. Certification mainly matters if you want to buy through Tesla's own install program.</p>
+<p>Tesla keeps its own list of certified installers on its website. Jaymarc Electric says on its site that it is a certified Tesla Wall Connector installer. That's the installer's own claim, so ask to see its Tesla certification when you call. Any licensed electrician can install a Wall Connector. Certification mainly matters if you want to buy through Tesla's own install program.</p>
 <h2 id="what-a-wall-connector-install-usually-involves">What a Wall Connector install usually involves</h2>
 <ul><li><strong>A dedicated 240-volt circuit.</strong> The Wall Connector can charge at up to 48 amps on a 60-amp breaker. Many homes use a lower setting on a 40- or 50-amp circuit to avoid a panel upgrade.</li><li><strong>A load calculation.</strong> The installer adds up your home's existing load to see how much room is left on your panel.</li><li><strong>Wi-Fi setup and commissioning.</strong> The charger is set to match the breaker using Tesla's app.</li><li><strong>A permit and inspection</strong> from your city or Greenville County.</li></ul>
